# Control Activations

This tutorial finds the processes behind a running Agent, drains it with `Jido.AgentServer.quiesce/2`, reopens it, and controls its idle timer.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "release/v3"}
])
```

## Start a warehouse Agent

`:count` holds its Turn until this notebook sends a message. The later cells use it to control an Agent with work in progress.

```elixir
defmodule ControlGuide.Count do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "control_guide_count",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{aisle: Jido.Joi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{aisle: aisle}, %{agent_state: state, notify: notify}) do
    send(notify, {:counting, self()})

    receive do
      :finish -> {:ok, %{state | counted: state.counted ++ [aisle]}}
    end
  end
end

defmodule ControlGuide.Warehouse do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "warehouse"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{counted: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/warehouse"
    route "aisle.count", ControlGuide.Count, as: :count

    route "aisle.note", as: :note do
      action %{aisle: aisle},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{aisle: Jido.Joi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | counted: context.agent_state.counted ++ [aisle]}}
      end
    end
  end
end

{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start(name: ControlGuide.Jido)
{:ok, warehouse} = Jido.start_agent(ControlGuide.Jido, ControlGuide.Warehouse, id: "north")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(warehouse)

start_count = fn aisle ->
  signal = ControlGuide.Warehouse.count_signal!(%{aisle: aisle})
  pending = Jido.AgentServer.submit(warehouse, signal, context: %{notify: self()})

  receive do
    {:counting, action} -> {pending, action}
  after
    1_000 -> raise "the Turn did not start"
  end
end
```

## Find the activation

An Agent started through an instance runs in an activation: a supervisor that owns the worker and everything tied to its lifetime. `Jido.AgentServer.activation/1` returns that supervisor, and `Jido.AgentServer.worker/1` goes the other way.

```elixir
activation = Jido.AgentServer.activation(warehouse)

{is_pid(activation), Jido.AgentServer.worker(activation) == warehouse, Jido.AgentServer.alive?(warehouse)}
# The cell returns {true, true, true}
```

[Agent Lifecycle](agent-activation-control.md) describes what the activation owns.

## Drain work with quiesce

`Jido.AgentServer.quiesce/2` closes admission so you can act on a settled Agent, for example before a deploy step. It does three things:

1. It closes admission. New Turns get `{:error, :quiesced}`.
2. It rejects every queued Turn with `{:error, :quiesced}`.
3. It waits for the active Turn and its effects to finish, then returns `:ok`.

```elixir
{active, action} = start_count.("a1")
queued = Jido.AgentServer.submit(warehouse, ControlGuide.Warehouse.note_signal!(%{aisle: "a2"}))

control = Task.async(fn -> Jido.AgentServer.quiesce(warehouse) end)

closing =
  Enum.find_value(1..100, fn _attempt ->
    case Jido.AgentServer.status(warehouse).admission.mode do
      :open ->
        Process.sleep(5)
        nil

      mode ->
        mode
    end
  end)

queued_result = Jido.AgentServer.await(queued)
new_result = Jido.AgentServer.call(warehouse, ControlGuide.Warehouse.note_signal!(%{aisle: "a3"}))

{closing, queued_result, new_result}
# The cell returns {:quiescing, {:error, :quiesced}, {:error, :quiesced}}
```

Reads keep working during the drain. The active Turn is still allowed to commit, so finish it and collect the result.

```elixir
send(action, :finish)
{:ok, _agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.await(active)
:ok = Task.await(control)

{Jido.AgentServer.status(warehouse).admission.mode, Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(warehouse).agent.state.counted}
# The cell returns {:quiesced, ["a1"]}
```

## Reopen admission

`Jido.AgentServer.resume/2` opens admission. Rejected requests do not come back, so their callers must send them again.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.resume(warehouse)

{:ok, agent, []} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(warehouse, ControlGuide.Warehouse.note_signal!(%{aisle: "a2"}))

agent.state.counted
# The cell returns ["a1", "a2"]
```

`resume/2` returns `{:error, :not_quiescent}` while the server is still draining. It returns `{:error, :unsafe_resume}` after an uncertain hibernation save. See [Hibernate and Thaw](hibernate-thaw-and-activate.md).

## Bound the drain

The `:timeout` option is the budget for the drain, with a default of 5,000 ms. It must be finite. On expiry, `quiesce/2` returns `{:error, :quiesce_timeout}` and admission stays closed. The server finishes its work and then settles as `:quiesced`, so you can call `resume/2` after that.

```elixir
{active, action} = start_count.("a4")

timed_out = Jido.AgentServer.quiesce(warehouse, timeout: 20)
still_draining = Jido.AgentServer.resume(warehouse)

send(action, :finish)
{:ok, _agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.await(active)

settled =
  Enum.find_value(1..100, fn _attempt ->
    case Jido.AgentServer.resume(warehouse) do
      :ok ->
        :reopened

      {:error, :not_quiescent} ->
        Process.sleep(5)
        nil
    end
  end)

{timed_out, still_draining, settled}
# The cell returns {{:error, :quiesce_timeout}, {:error, :not_quiescent}, :reopened}
```

A second control request during a drain returns `{:error, :control_in_progress}`. Owned work, such as an Action in a Turn, gets `{:error, :reentrant_turn}`. During startup the result is `{:error, :not_ready}`.

## Cancel instead of waiting

`active: :cancel` cancels the active Turn if it has not reached commit. A save or effects already in progress still finish.

```elixir
{active, _action} = start_count.("a5")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.quiesce(warehouse, active: :cancel)
result = Jido.AgentServer.await(active)
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.resume(warehouse)

result
# The cell returns {:error, :cancelled}
```

## Stop an idle Agent

Set `:idle_timeout` to stop an Agent that has no work. The default is `:infinity`, which disables the timer. The budget starts when the server is settled, admission is open, and no owner is attached.

```elixir
{:ok, kiosk} = Jido.start_agent(ControlGuide.Jido, ControlGuide.Warehouse, id: "kiosk", idle_timeout: 50)
monitor = Process.monitor(kiosk)

receive do
  {:DOWN, ^monitor, :process, ^kiosk, reason} -> reason
after
  1_000 -> :still_running
end
# The cell returns {:shutdown, :idle_timeout}
```

The worker exits with a shutdown reason, so the activation stops and does not restart. With `idle_policy: :hibernate`, it saves before it stops. That policy needs persistence.

## Keep an Agent alive

These functions change the idle timer:

- `Jido.AgentServer.attach/3` adds an owner process, which prevents idle expiry. Attaching twice has the same effect as once.
- `Jido.AgentServer.detach/3` removes an owner. The last detach starts a new idle budget. An owner that exits counts as a detach.
- `Jido.AgentServer.touch/1` restarts the idle budget. It does not open closed admission.

Attach a process that represents a user session, such as a LiveView, and let its exit release the Agent.

```elixir
{:ok, session} = Jido.start_agent(ControlGuide.Jido, ControlGuide.Warehouse, id: "session", idle_timeout: 50)
monitor = Process.monitor(session)

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.attach(session)
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.attach(session)
owners = Jido.AgentServer.status(session).owner_count

Process.sleep(100)
alive_while_attached = Process.alive?(session)

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.detach(session)

stopped =
  receive do
    {:DOWN, ^monitor, :process, ^session, reason} -> reason
  after
    1_000 -> :still_running
  end

{owners, alive_while_attached, stopped}
# The cell returns {1, true, {:shutdown, :idle_timeout}}
```

Owners and closed admission both survive a worker restart inside the activation, so a recovered worker does not reopen an Agent you closed.

